<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gelou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 900</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gelou is a ruler described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, written around 1200, as a Vlach duke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> at the time of the Hungarian conquest around 900. According to the chronicle he was defeated by the Hungarian chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuhutum">Tuhutum</a> and killed while fleeing, after which his people accepted Tuhutum as their lord. No other source mentions him, and historians dispute whether he existed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gelou.jpg" alt="Gelou" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Țetcu Mircea Rareș · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilauCJ2013_(4).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Everything said of Gelou comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, the earliest surviving Hungarian chronicle, by an unidentified author known as Anonymus. The chronicle leaves out the opponents of the conquering Hungarians known from contemporary sources, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svatopluk-i-of-moravia">Svatopluk I of Moravia</a>, and instead tells of battles with local rulers, Gelou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> and Salan, named nowhere else. It describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, the land beyond the woods, as rich in salt and gold-bearing rivers, inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>. Archaeology shows a people who cremated their dead living around the salt mines from the seventh to the ninth centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Anonymus calls Gelou a certain Vlach and duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>, a ruler who was not steadfast and had no good warriors about him, his people armed only with bows and arrows. After the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a>, the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuhutum">Tuhutum</a> sent a spy east of the Meseș Gates and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>'s leave, crossed the forest in one day. Gelou met him on the Almaș River, but part of Tuhutum's army crossed upstream and surprised him. Gelou fled towards his castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somes">Someș</a> and was killed on the Căpuș River, near the later village of Gilău. His people swore loyalty to Tuhutum at a place Anonymus calls Eskellő, from the Hungarian word for oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Most Romanian historians, among them Vlad Georgescu, Alexandru Madgearu and Victor Spinei, hold the account reliable in substance; Madgearu writes that Gelou was a real person, though the story mixes oral tradition with invented facts. Most Hungarian historians, among them István Bóna, Pál Engel and Gyula Kristó, see him as one of several imaginary figures named after places, here the village of Gilău. Excavations at Dăbâca, long taken for his seat, have not proved a fortress there before 900. Dennis Deletant judges that the case for or against his existence cannot be proven.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gelou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It describes <b>Gelou</b> as a Vlach ruling Vlachs and Slavs in the Someș valley of Transylvania at the time of the Hungarian conquest, with his centre usually placed at Dăbâca.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-gelou">Duchy of Gelou</a></li></ul></section>
